note F47: CPython Lib/hmac.py, HMAC.update

note GAP: interfaces and dynamic dispatch - _hmac (a C-accelerated HMAC object) and _inner (a hashlib hash object) are different classes that both have an update method, and the call is dispatched on whichever one is present; the doc has no interface or method declaration, so Hasher stands for "anything with update"
type Hmac
    note WORKAROUND: Python uses None for "no fast HMAC object"; here it is a maybe field
    field fast_hmac as a maybe Hasher
    field inner as a maybe Hasher
    field outer as a maybe Hasher

to update_hasher hasher with msg
    given hasher is an editable Hasher
    given msg is Bytes

to update hmac with msg
    note UNCLEAR: the docstring says "Feed data from msg into this hashing object"; Python accepts any bytes-like object (bytes, bytearray, memoryview), and the doc has only Bytes
    given hmac is an editable Hmac
    given msg is Bytes
    note WORKAROUND: in Python `self._hmac or self._inner` relies on truthiness, and when neither is set the call fails with AttributeError on None; Scribe has no truthiness, so both are matched explicitly and the impossible case becomes a named failure
    fails with NoHashState

    note WORKAROUND: `inst = a or b` followed by one call becomes a nested choose; changing a value held inside a maybe field needs it bound by the choose, and the doc does not say whether that binding is editable in place
    note UNCLEAR: whether `when some h` on an editable field binds h by reference so that update_hasher changes the hasher inside hmac, or binds a copy
    choose fast_hmac of hmac
        when some h
            update_hasher h with msg
        when nothing
            choose inner of hmac
                when some h
                    update_hasher h with msg
                when nothing
                    fail with NoHashState
